=== component-traceability v2.1 (sequence 3) cross-batch overlap fix evidence ===
Committed on the same local three-organisation Fabric test-network used
throughout this repository, replacing v2.0/sequence 2. This run demonstrates
a fix for a real gap identified after the v2.0 hardening pass: a product
indexed under two different batches (Section 3.3, RecordAssembly) could be
recalled by each batch independently, but RecallStatus was a single field
with no record of which batch's campaign actually owned the current recall.
RevokeRecall(batchID) cleared RecallStatus on every token reachable from
batchID's index entries, including a product still genuinely under a
DIFFERENT, unrelated batch's open recall -- silently clearing a recall the
call had no authority over.

Fix: a new RecallBatchID field records which batch's TriggerRecall call set
the current RecallStatus. TriggerRecall no longer relabels a token already
recalled under a different batch (reporting it separately as
skippedOverlap); ReviseRecallReason and RevokeRecall now only touch tokens
whose RecallBatchID matches the batch named in the call.

This run: PRODUCT-OV is assembled from COMP-OV1 (BATCH-OV-X) and COMP-OV2
(BATCH-OV-Y). BATCH-OV-X recalls it first (recallBatchId becomes
BATCH-OV-X). BATCH-OV-Y's later, independent TriggerRecall call reports
PRODUCT-OV under skippedOverlap rather than relabelling it. RevokeRecall on
the WRONG batch (BATCH-OV-Y) leaves PRODUCT-OV's recall untouched
(recallStatus still RECALLED, recallBatchId still BATCH-OV-X) -- this is the
exact bug being fixed. RevokeRecall on the correct batch (BATCH-OV-X) then
clears it, with lifecycle status (ASSEMBLED) preserved throughout.

Console output below is unedited peer chaincode invoke/query output against
the real network, captured in a single run.
